==Abstract==

This document describes the process to use swapped UTXOs for inputs, [[bip-0352.mediawiki|BIP 352]] silent payment addresses for outputs, and hide the transacted amount with multiple interpretations.

==Motivation==

Payjoin requires coordination and sharing of UTXOs between sender and recipient. This affects privacy and UX in some cases. Octojoin avoids sharing of UTXOs and uses swapped UTXOs along with other inputs from wallet in the transaction. 
This reduces coordination and there is no need for recipient to share their UTXOs. Users can still achieve same level of privacy and the transaction looks normal on-chain.

====Relation to Monero====

Most Monero transactions use 16 ring size for the input, making it impossible to determine which input is signed by the user out of 16. The amounts are hidden, and stealth addresses are used for outputs.

Octojoin achieves partial obfuscation similar to Monero's ring signatures ([https://www.getmonero.org/2024/04/27/fcmps.html FCMP++] will replace ring signatures in the future) and stealth addresses. Swaps can be done off-chain to obscure the transaction's input ownership and history.

==Specification==

The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "MAY" and "RECOMMENDED" in this document are to be interpreted as described in RFC 2119.

====Sequence Diagram====

<source lang="mermaid">
sequenceDiagram
    participant Alice
    participant Wallet
    participant Bob

    Alice->>Wallet: Label swapped UTXOs as 'octojoin'
    Bob->>Alice: Share silent payment address
    Alice->>Wallet: Create transaction using 'octojoin' UTXOs + 1 normal input
    Note right of Alice: Minimum 2 outputs and avoid change for multiple interpretations
    Wallet->>Bob: Pay Bob
</source>

Users should label some UTXOs as “octojoin” so that they can be used in octojoin transactions. Ideally, these UTXOs should be ones that have been swapped with others and are linked to someone else’s transaction history.

'''Input Obfuscation with Off-Chain Swaps'''

Do off-chain swaps (e.g. statechain, submarine swaps, or coinswap) to obtain UTXOs with different histories. Ensure that all inputs come from other users except one belonging to the sender, making it difficult for on-chain analysts to determine ownership.

'''Outputs'''

Silent payment ([[bip-0352.mediawiki|BIP 352]]) addresses are RECOMMENDED for the outputs: the sender derives a fresh, unlinkable output address for each payment output with no interaction from the recipient. Implementations MAY instead accept multiple plain recipient addresses, but each MUST be a fresh address that is not otherwise linkable to the recipient; reusing or linking those addresses defeats the unlinkability that silent payments provide automatically.

'''Multiple Outputs and Different Interpretations'''

The transaction includes multiple outputs, which may include change to the sender, hiding the payment amount.

An algorithm requiring some values from the user to create the transaction or notify the user about missing inputs will be implemented by different wallets:

* Payment amount
* Number of inputs (default: 3)
* Number of outputs (default: 2)
* Equal amounts in inputs (default: false)
* Equal amounts in outputs (default: false)

''Equal amounts in inputs'' controls whether the selected inputs are constrained to equal (or near-equal) values, where the largest input is at most 10% larger than the smallest. It is disabled by default.

''Equal amounts in outputs'' splits the payment into outputs of equal value instead of at random. It is disabled by default.

Analysis of different decoy selection algorithms used in Monero: https://github.com/monero-project/research-lab/issues/99

Input selection is important in Octojoin, although not the same as decoy selection used in different Monero wallets. Recommendations for Octojoin algorithms:

Transaction MUST have at least 3 inputs, all swapped except one. It SHOULD avoid mixed input types and use random wallet fingerprints, including BIP 69. It SHOULD use inputs of similar age. Some inputs SHOULD be recent UTXOs. All outputs, including the change output, MUST use the same script type; when silent payment outputs are used (P2TR) the change output MUST also be P2TR.

====Splitting the Amount====

The payment amount is split into one output per recipient address, as many as the requested number of outputs (default 2). The split is random, so that no output shows the amount and no output is a round value that marks it as a payment. It is made after the inputs are selected, because one of its rules depends on the change.

All values are in satoshis. Let P be the payment amount, k the number of payment outputs and D the dust threshold, which is the largest dust threshold of the payment output script types (294 sat for P2WPKH and 330 sat for P2TR at the default dust relay fee). Implementations MAY use a higher dust threshold, such as the 546 sat that Electrum uses for every script type. Let

 lo = max(D + 1, ceil(P / (2k)))
 hi = floor(3P / (2k))

so that every payment output is between half and one and a half times an even share of the payment. A value is round when it is a multiple of 1,000 sat.

The payment output values x<sub>1</sub>, ..., x<sub>k</sub> MUST

* add up to P,
* each lie between lo and hi inclusive,
* be different from each other and from the change, and
* not be round.

No part of the amount may be dropped or moved to the fee. A payment of at most D sat, or of less than k(D + 1) + k(k - 1)/2 sat, cannot be split this way and MUST be rejected.

Implementations SHOULD pick uniformly at random among the splits that follow these rules and the rule for the change in the next section, using a cryptographically secure source of randomness. The following procedure does so.

# Let R = P - k * lo.
# Draw k - 1 integers uniformly at random between 0 and R inclusive, and sort them as r<sub>1</sub> <= ... <= r<sub>k-1</sub>. Let r<sub>0</sub> = 0 and r<sub>k</sub> = R.
# Let x<sub>i</sub> = lo + r<sub>i</sub> - r<sub>i-1</sub> for i from 1 to k.
# If the values break any of the rules, draw again.

For example, 0.003 BTC in 2 outputs gives two values between 0.00075 and 0.00225 BTC that add up to 0.003 BTC, such as 0.00131847 and 0.00168153 BTC. The same amount in 3 outputs gives three values between 0.0005 and 0.0015 BTC.

Implementations MAY derive the randomness from a wallet secret and the payment request, so that planning the same payment again gives the same transaction and the user signs what was shown.

With equal amounts in outputs, every payment output gets floor(P / k), and when P does not divide evenly, P mod k of them get 1 sat more. The rules on different and round values do not apply, and P MUST be at least k(D + 1). A change output next to equal payment outputs is the one output with a different value, so implementations SHOULD prefer selections without a change output, as above, and SHOULD warn the user when every selection leaves change.

====Input Selection and the Unnecessary Input Heuristic====

The unnecessary input heuristic (UIH) flags a transaction in which an input could be removed while the remaining inputs still cover the payment and the fee. Ordinary wallets do not add such an input, so an unnecessary input shows that the transaction is not a simple payment.

Because Octojoin deliberately adds swapped inputs, naive selection can trip this heuristic. For n inputs funding a payment P with fee F, no input is unnecessary when

 (sum of all inputs) - min(inputs) < P + F

which holds exactly when the change is smaller than every input, or when there is no change output.

Implementations SHOULD consider every selection of numInputs - 1 swapped coins and one other coin that funds the payment, and prefer them in this order.

# Selections without a change output, which leave no change to find and no unnecessary input.
# Selections whose change is smaller than every input and lies between lo and hi, so that no input is unnecessary and the change has the size of a payment output.
# Selections whose change is smaller than every input.
# Selections whose change lies between lo and hi.
# Any other selection that funds the payment.

With equal amounts in inputs, selections whose inputs are near-equal come before all of these, and every swapped coin is a candidate rather than only the smallest ones. Implementations SHOULD warn the user when no selection has near-equal inputs.

Implementations SHOULD pick at random among the selections of the best rank. A fixed choice such as the smallest change would make the change smaller than the payment outputs most of the time.

The change MUST NOT be round. When it would be, implementations move 1 sat from the change to the fee. A change at or below the dust threshold is not created, and its value goes to the fee. The change uses a fresh address of the same script type as the payment outputs.

The same heuristic is used to find the change. In a transaction where every input is needed, an observer takes an output that is smaller than every input for the change, and the smallest output when there are several of them (UIH1 in [https://eprint.iacr.org/2022/589.pdf Unnecessary Input Heuristics & PayJoin Transactions]). When the change is smaller than every input, the split SHOULD put at least one payment output below every input as well, so that the change is not the only output the heuristic can point to. The split is drawn at random within that rule, so the heuristic points at a payment output or at the change depending on where the values fall.

When no selection avoids an unnecessary input, or the change cannot be made to blend in, implementations SHOULD warn the user before paying.

====Fee Estimation====

The fee is derived from the virtual size of the constructed transaction:

 fee = ceil(vsize * feeRate)

where vsize is estimated as overhead + (numInputs * input vbytes) + (numOutputs * output vbytes), and numOutputs includes the change output when one is present. The exact per-input and per-output vbyte constants depend on the script type and are implementation specific. The fee is 1 sat higher when the change would otherwise be round, and includes the whole remainder when there is no change output.

==Rationale==

====Default Values====

We need at least 1 normal input in the transaction, so 2 swapped inputs will provide minimum privacy in a transaction. 1 output will reveal the payment amount, at least 2 outputs are required for multiple interpretations.

Total transactions with 3 inputs (~43,000,000): https://blockchair.com/bitcoin/transactions?s=time(desc)&q=input_count(3)#f=hash,input_count,time

Total transaction with 3 inputs and 2 outputs (~27,000,000): https://blockchair.com/bitcoin/transactions?s=time(desc)&q=input_count(3),output_count(2)#f=hash,input_count,time,output_count

<source lang="mermaid">
xychart-beta
    title "Transactions with 3 inputs and 2 outputs"
    x-axis [Jan, Feb, Mar, Apr, May, Jun, Jul, Aug, Sep]
    y-axis "Transactions (in thousands)" 0 --> 160 
    bar [125, 143, 157, 130, 117, 106, 121, 123, 127]
</source>

Transactions that already use 3 inputs and 2 outputs will serve as the anonymity set for octojoin transactions with default values. It is possible to change defaults and use a higher number of inputs and outputs.

====Random Split====

Earlier drafts split the amount into standard denominations from 0.001 to 1 BTC. Those outputs are round, and chain analysis takes round outputs for payments, which leaves the change as the only output that is not round. An amount with fewer denominations than outputs, such as 0.002 BTC in 2 outputs, was also cut into equal values, and two equal outputs next to a third show which outputs are the payment ([https://github.com/octojoin/bip/issues/4 issue 4]). Implementations also differed in how they filled the outputs in that case.

A random split into values of similar size that are not round removes both signals, and the rules for the change make it look like one more payment output. Together with the rule on unnecessary inputs and one script type for every output, this keeps the heuristics that payjoin ([[bip-0078.mediawiki|BIP 78]]) breaks, common input ownership and change found by its script type or a round amount, from working on Octojoin transactions either.

The range from half to one and a half even shares keeps every output well above dust and keeps one output from carrying most of the payment, while leaving many possible values. With 2 outputs, either one can be anything from 25% to 75% of the amount.

Equal amounts in outputs stay available as an option for users who want them. Next to a change output, equal outputs show which output is the change, so the option comes with a preference for selections without change and a warning when there is change.

====Swapped UTXOs====

Swapping UTXOs off-chain will obscure the transaction's input ownership and history. It also adds more noise on-chain to make chain analysis difficult.

====Silent Payment Addresses====

A sender may require more than 1 output, so silent payment addresses are the best approach to generate new addresses with no interaction and maximum privacy.

==Implementation==

Proof of Concept: https://github.com/octojoin/poc

==Backwards Compatibility==

This is a new protocol and has no compatibility issues. It RECOMMENDS [[bip-0352.mediawiki|BIP 352]] silent payments for the outputs, but it can also be used with multiple fresh, unlinked recipient addresses. It is not a replacement for payjoin (BIP 77 and BIP 78) and benefits from multiple interpretations on-chain.

==Attack Vectors==

* Metadata leak in swaps
* User not careful while spending outputs created in Octojoin transaction
* Round payment amounts. The payment outputs add up to the payment amount, so when it is round, adding up sets of outputs can find the payment outputs and with them the change. Recipients SHOULD request amounts that are not round.

